WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.750
INTRO:
Nike is the largest sports company in the

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:03.890
world by far.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:08.770
In fact, they’re so dominant that they’re
5 times larger than the second-largest sports

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.759
brand in the world, Adidas, coming in at $262
billion.

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:18.350
This means that Nike is larger than even Toyota
and Coca-Cola.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.660
While the magnitude of their success is super
impressive, it’s not too crazy when you

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.840
consider that Nike’s shoes and clothing
are basically ubiquitous.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.370
But despite the ubiquitous nature of Nike,
most people have no clue who’s behind the

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.490
company.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.970
This is actually quite ironic given that Phil
Knight’s tenure at Nike is longer than the

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.390
entire existence of Apple and Microsoft.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.510
On top of this, Phil is the 19th richest person
in the world with a net worth of $62 billion.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:50.420
So, here’s the story of the mystery man
behind Nike.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:56.010
PHIL KNIGHT:
Taking a look back, Phil Knight was born on

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.970
February 24, 1938, in Portland, Oregon.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:04.129
Phil’s father, Bill, was originally a lawyer
by trade, but he had since opted to run his

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.490
own newspaper publishing business called the
Oregon Journal.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:11.200
Bill was quite a strict man who cared a lot
about hard work and discipline.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.240
He didn’t like the idea of handouts and
thought that everyone needs to learn to fend

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.639
for themselves.

00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:21.369
This was made quite clear when he refused
to give Phil a summer job at the Oregan Journal.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:25.159
Since he couldn’t get a job at his father’s
company, Phil decided to apply for a job at

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:28.319
Oregonian, who was his father’s biggest
rival.

00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:32.109
At Oregonian, Phil worked the morning shift
where he tabulated sports scores.

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:36.439
The job wasn’t too bad, but the same could
not be said about the commute.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:40.280
Phil had to run the full seven miles each
way to get to work and back.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.710
Fortunately, though, Phil wasn’t just a
great runner but he was a world-class runner.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.289
His personal best at the mile was just 4 minutes
and 13 seconds.

00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:52.329
Moving into college, Phil would attend the
University of Oregon in Eugene where he was

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:55.570
a star athlete.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.729
In 1957, 58, and 59, Phil would win varsity
letters for his track performance, and he

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:02.679
would get the opportunity to be trained by
the legendary Bill Bowerman.

00:02:02.679 --> 00:02:08.020
Throughout his career, Bill would train 31
Olympic athletes, 22 NCAA champions, and 16

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.129
sub-4-minute milers, so clearly Bowerman knew
what he was doing.

00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:15.939
Similar to Phil’s father, Bowerman pushed
Phil to always challenge the limits and never

00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:16.939
settle for average.

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:21.159
Though it was easy for Phil to apply these
principles to running, it wasn’t so easy

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:22.159
with academics.

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:26.370
It’s not that Phil was a bad student or
anything; he was just confused as to what

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.830
he wanted to do.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.209
He had been majoring in Journalism probably
inspired by his father, but he was having

00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:35.500
second doubts about whether that’s what
he really wanted to do.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.200
So, after graduation in 1959, Phil went ahead
and joined the army.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.120
He completed a year of service before he decided
that he wanted to return to school enrolling

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.010
in Stanford’s MBA program.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.000
While completing his MBA, Phil would write
a paper called “Can Japanese Sports Shoes

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.390
Do to German Sports Shoes What Japanese Cameras
Did to German Cameras?”.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.920
And this would become the blueprint for Nike.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.540
BLUE RIBBON SPORTS:
Phil didn’t just jump fully into selling

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.310
shoes after college though.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:08.180
Instead, Phil opted to take the CPA exam and
join a local accounting firm in Portland.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.950
In the background though, Phil was hard at
work testing out different designs and securing

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.160
contractors.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.859
In November of 1962, Phil traveled to Japan
where he discovered something called the Tiger-brand

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:20.610
shoe manufactured by Onitsuka.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.660
Phil was thoroughly impressed by the high
quality and low cost of the shoes and would

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.109
form a licensing deal with the company.

00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:30.670
After he returned to the US, Phil would reach
out to his former coach, Bill Bowerman, to

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:31.999
get his opinion on the shoe.

00:03:31.999 --> 00:03:35.969
And to Phil’s surprise, Bill not only loved
the shoes, but he was willing to join the

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:37.640
business as a partner.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.079
Phil of course took Bill up on that offer
and the duo started Blue Ribbon Sports in

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:43.310
January of 1964.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.670
Neither Phil nor Bill had any high-level business
connections or enough money to market their

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.670
product.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.580
So, they decided to market their shoes the
old-fashioned way.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.159
The duo started off by attending every track
meet in the area and attempting to sell their

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:56.600
shoes to the athletes.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.329
This was moderately successful and they were
able to sell 1,300 pairs by the end of the

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:03.810
first year grossing a total of $8,000 worth
of sales.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.389
In their second year of business, the duo
onboarded a full-time employee who helped

00:04:07.389 --> 00:04:11.150
them attend more track meets and over double
sales to $20,000.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.730
And this gave them the confidence to open
up a physical location in Santa Monica, California.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:20.480
By 1967, sales were doing so well that Phil
would open another store on the east coast

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.710
in Wellesley, Massachusetts.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.260
About 2 years into running both stores, Phil
would finally quit his accounting job and

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.560
go all-in on the business.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.670
This seemed like a great decision for 2 years,
but in 1971, the company would run into a

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:34.670
massive roadblock.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:39.190
They had a dispute with their manufacturer
Onitsuka and two would choose to go separate

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:40.210
ways.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.861
Given that Blue Ribbon Sports no longer had
the rights to sell Onitsuka’s shoes, Phil

00:04:43.861 --> 00:04:50.300
and Bill were forced to create their own shoes
and that brings us to Nike.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.190
NIKE:
Given Phil’s experience as an athlete himself,

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:57.560
he decided to design the shoes based on what
he would want as a runner.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.040
He also consulted with other runners such
as long-distance runner Steve Prefontaine

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.260
to get feedback on the shows and improve the
design.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.960
Most news sources report that it was Phil
who designed the company’s first shoes,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:12.150
but an Olympic athlete named Otis Davis actually
suggests that it was actually Bill.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.260
Otis was one of the first people to try the
new shoes and he says that he physically witnessed

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.770
Bill make the shoes with a waffle iron.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.500
In terms of his experience with the shoes,
Otis says that he didn’t particularly like

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.600
how they felt on his feet as the shoes had
no support and were too tight.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.890
So, clearly, Bill and Phil had some work to
do, but they would figure it out over the

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:31.890
next few years.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.810
In terms of branding for the shoe, the duo
didn’t want to use the Blue Ribbon Sports

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.800
brand as it wasn’t that catchy or memorable.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.390
When they were brainstorming new branding
ideas, an employee named Jeff Johnson would

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.900
suggest the name Nike which is the name of
the Greek winged goddess of victory.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:51.090
Around the same time, Phil reached out to
a graphic design student named Carolyn Davidson,

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.350
and he paid her $35 to create a logo for the
new brand.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:59.030
Carolyn came up with a couple of logo designs,
but Phil wasn’t a fan of any of them.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.151
Carolyn offered to modify the designs and
come up with some new designs as well, but

00:06:03.151 --> 00:06:06.470
Phil didn’t want to delay the manufacturing
process any further.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:11.140
So, he simply settled with the swoosh logo
stating that maybe it’ll grow on him.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:15.680
Ironically, the swoosh logo has turned into
one of the most iconic and recognizable logos

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:16.680
in the world.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.120
Anyway, Nike’s first track shoe, the Cortez,
made its debut at the 1972 Olympic trials,

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.050
and it didn’t take long for the shoe to
spread like wildfire.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:30.300
MEGA CORPORATION:
Runners around the world wanted to try out

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.750
this new shoe put out by Bill Bowerman, and
once they tried it, they got addicted.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.250
Within just 8 years, Nike went from launching
their first shoe to controlling 50% of the

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:40.319
athletic shoe market.

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:44.050
Nike became such a large part of Blue Ribbon
Sports that Phil decided to rename the company

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.590
itself Nike in 1978.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.500
And in 1980, Phil would take the company public.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.170
Bill slowly cashed out throughout the 1980s
and started to step away from the business

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:54.870
as he was getting quite old.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:59.710
But Phil was just getting started and his
next order of business was securing endorsements.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:04.080
One of Nike’s first endorsement deals was
with the tennis superstar John McEnroe.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.990
John had recently hurt his ankle and was taking
some time off.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.410
But when he finally returned, he was wearing
Nike shoes which exploded the popularity of

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.510
Nike amongst the Tennis world.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:18.160
Up until then, Nike shoes were mainly purchased
by runners, but after this positive experience

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.670
with John, Phil realized that he could use
other sports stars to break into those sports

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:22.670
as well.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.770
This of course leads us to the partnerships
with Michael Jordan, Andre Agassi, Charles

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:28.100
Barkley, and Tiger Woods.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:33.720
In the 1990s, Nike expanded into hockey, golf,
and soccer, and by the end of the decade,

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.500
they would achieve over $10 billion in annual
sales.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.350
Unfortunately, Bill passed away at the end
of 1999 right before the turn of the century,

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:45.690
but there’s no doubt that he lived a pretty
solid life stretching 88 years.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:49.480
Anyway, Nike was also making several acquisitions
to aid in their growth.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:54.040
For example, in 1988, they bought Cole Haan
which they eventually sell in 2012.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:58.780
Similarly, they bought Bauer in 1994 and sold
it in 2008.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.230
But by far their most famous acquisition didn’t
come till 2003 when they purchased Converse.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:07.770
Anyway, moving into the new century, Nike
has placed a strong emphasis on sports technology.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:11.820
They started selling heart rate monitors,
high altitude wrist compasses, and they partnered

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.650
with companies like Apple.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:17.940
All of this has secured Nike’s position
as the dominant sports player in the new tech-filled

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.830
world, but the same cannot be said about Phil
Knight.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:26.150
PHIL KNIGHT TODAY:
There’s no question that Phil is quite a

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.150
private man who avoids the spotlight as much
as possible.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.820
I’m sure his value for privacy plays a large
role in this, but I think there are also some

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:37.390
other factors driving him away from the public’s
view starting with nepotism.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.680
Unlike Phil’s father who refused to give
Phil a job at his own company, Phil goes out

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.250
of his way to help his children through their
careers.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.340
Take his son Travis for example.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:50.990
After an unsuccessful attempt at becoming
a rapper, Travis decided to become an animator.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:56.270
In 1998, Phil would buy 15% of an animation
studio named Vinton Studios, and Travis would

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:59.210
get a job there shortly after as an animator.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:03.500
A few years later, Phil would buy the entire
studio, rename it Laika, and appoint Travis

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.770
to the new board.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.980
On top of this, Phil would invest $180 million
into the company to help them produce their

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.150
first stop motion film called Coraline, and
after the launch of the film, Travis would

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:15.580
become CEO and president of the new company.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:19.650
I don’t know about you, but it looks to
me like Phil used his influence and money

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.660
to transition Travis from being a failed rapper
to being the CEO of an animation studio.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:28.750
Now, of course, Phil didn’t break any laws
or do anything illegal, but this isn’t a

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.550
particularly good look.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:34.670
Aside from nepotism, Phil doesn’t have the
best of records when it comes to human rights.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:39.640
Since the 1990s, Nike has been pummeled with
human rights concerns and poor treatment of

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:40.640
employees.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.950
There are reports suggesting that Nike workers
in Vietnam were earning just 61 to 89 cents

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:50.830
per hour in 2016, and the use of corporal
punishment is allegedly common in their factories.

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:55.110
Here in the US, many activists are complaining
that Amazon doesn’t pay enough, and let’s

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:59.770
not forget, Amazon has a minimum wage of $15
per hour for all US employees.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:03.750
Yet, Jeff Bezos is one of the most hated billionaires
out there.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:07.820
Compared to Jeff Bezos, Phil Knight’s popularity
doesn’t even make it onto the charts.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.640
Imagine if Phil was as popular as Jeff; he’d
be torched alive by the media for paying employees

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:14.500
less than a dollar per hour.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.080
And I think we’d agree that it’s better
to not be known at all than to have a negative

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.380
public perception like Jeff Bezos.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:25.130
Aside from paying low wages and nepotism,
Phil is also notorious for extreme lobbying

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:26.150
efforts.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:29.529
Over the years, Phil has spent millions of
dollars on political donations to Republican

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:31.180
candidates in Oregon.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:35.560
Similarly, he was the single largest donor
for the campaign against Oregon Ballot Measures

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:37.240
66 and 67.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.630
This legislation was supposed to increase
income tax on corporations and high-income

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:41.630
individuals.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:45.360
Now, I’m not saying that Phil should pay
more taxes or that he’s wrong for supporting

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.750
the Republican party.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.940
The fundamental problem isn’t the policies
that he’s supporting.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:53.731
The fundamental problem is that he’s using
his money to influence the government, and

00:10:53.731 --> 00:10:57.050
I think both sides can agree that this is
wrong.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.910
Considering all this, maybe Phil’s relatively
small public image isn’t due to his modesty

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:05.280
or privacy, but because he doesn’t want
to attract attention towards his frowned-upon

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.420
activities.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.260
But that’s just speculation.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.120
Why do you guys think Phil is so private?

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:11.120
Comment that down below.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.130
Also, drop a like if you’re a fan of Nike’s
products but wish that their business was

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:16.130
a lot cleaner.

